[{:seon.task/name "type-findings-are-an-evals-warnings"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: Jolt checks the success types of calls (`jolt.passes.types/check-form`, non-strict by default). It takes a unit first: `(jolt.passes.types/check-form (jolt.passes.types/new-unit) node)`; with the node alone it throws an arity error. Ran 2026-11-06 at a root and in an agent, on nodes from `seon.program/tap`: `(first 42)` answers `[{:op \"first\" :argpos 0 :type \"a number\" :pos {:line 1 :column 1} :msg \"`first` requires a seqable, but argument 1 is a number\"}]` (:op is a string), `(defn f [] (first 42))` the same with the inner call's :pos, and `(+ 1 2)` and `(defn g [x] (inc x))` answer `[]`. :pos is a position in the request's text (a call on line 4 of the request says :line 4), where an error's :seon.error/at is in the form's own terms (`seon.repl/located`)., but only prints them to stderr when the environment variable JOLT_CHECK is set (host/chez/compile-eval.ss); stderr reaches an agent only after its death, so an eval never shows them. Our REPL already holds each analyzed node: `seon.repl/wrap-record` binds `jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*` to `seon.program/tap`, which is called with [form node], and `seon.repl/response` builds a response's `:warnings` from keys of its answers (:instrumented, :not-program, :shadowed), each entry `{:seon.repl/finding K ...}`. The nodes reach the wrap's around-reply as `(:nodes l)`, also for a form that threw, so `(first 42)`, which answers only its :ex today, can answer both. `(eval-in \"(first 42)\")` in seon.repl-test goes through this path. Correct: a small step of the same wrap calls `check-form` on each node the tap holds and puts its findings on the answer for `seon.repl/response` to add to `:warnings` as data (no environment variable, nothing refused: it records, AGENTS §4), rendered with the item. Measured silent on all of `src` (3,397 forms, 0 findings: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics); not measured yet: the cost per form, owed as a cost proof of work done. Gate: the owner's go, since it adds content to an eval item (AGENTS §3 root's gate, 4). Deletes nothing; it replaces the idea of setting JOLT_CHECK. Jolt's JOLT_DIAG (the CLI's uncaught-error report), JOLT_DEBUG (build and cache diagnostics) and its nREPL server are deliberately unused: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-provably-wrong-call-is-a-warning-on-its-item seon.repl-test/a-checked-form-costs-its-nodes seon.repl-test/our-own-source-loads-with-no-type-warnings}}
 {:seon.task/name "err-text-is-the-err-stream"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: what a form writes to `*err*` is already its own key, an answer's :err-out and a response's :err (ran 2026-11-06: `(binding [*out* *err*] (println \"to err\"))` answers `:err`). The answer's :err-text is left: `(str err (nrepl/err->msg v {:outer outer}))` in `seon.repl/eval-code`, `(nrepl/err->msg form {:phase :read-source})` at its read error, and `(nrepl/err->msg e)` in `seon.repl/eval-string`'s catch. Its only reader is `seon.repl/response`, as the message of an error it makes when an answer has no :err; no test reads it. The error's own render is `seon.render.ai/error` and its data carries the position in the form's terms (:seon.error/at). Correct: the three producers, that reader's fallback and `seon.repl/outer-frames`, which feeds only the first, are deleted. Replaces a second render by the one. The proof: `(find-var 'seon.repl/outer-frames)` is nil, red on main, and `a-response-carries-values-not-their-printed-text`, which already passes, stays green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-response-carries-values-not-their-printed-text seon.repl-test/an-evals-answer-has-one-error-render}}
 {:seon.task/name "records-keep-the-exact-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Storage keeps the exact value; renders budget it (AGENTS.md section 9: a clipping function). Left in src: `seon.root/end-agent` records a dead agent's :stderr as its last :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes though the file (`seon.agent/stderr-path`) is kept beside the store. Gone already: `seon.agent/error-map`'s message-chars cut, seon.model's 300-char HTTP error body, seon.learn's 80-char form and seon.agent-proc's 200-char form in :last-op and :running. Correct: each record holds the whole value or a reference to it (the stderr file's path and size); the context and the dashboard render them under their budgets through the one printer. Deletes root's use of `seon.lib/tail-bytes` in `seon.root/end-agent` and :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes as seon.root reads it (max-assertions is gone: every failing assertion is kept). `seon.lib/tail-bytes` itself stays: `seon.watch`, `seon.vector` and `seon.fx/stall-evidence` read it, and seon.watch reads the same key, for a watched process's death, which this plan does not decide."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-exception-message-is-recorded-whole
                      seon.problems-test/a-red-assertion-is-persisted
                      seon.problems-test/a-death-record-points-at-the-whole-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-repl-fallback-keeps-its-forms-position"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by batch 87's review (the REPL's names in every namespace, seon.agent/repl-name over Jolt's jolt.host/*resolve-unresolved*). Follow-ups in Jolt, on a jolt/<topic> branch of seantempesta/jolt; each ran 2026-11-06 in an agent. (1) jolt.analyzer/analyze-list*'s fallback arm analyzes (cons fallback (rest items)) without the form's metadata, which is still so in reference-code/jolt, but no lost position was seen: `(source 1 2 3 4)` on line 4 of a request answers :clojure.error/line 4 :column 1, and `(apropos (no-such-thing 1))` on line 3 answers its inner position. It needs an input that shows a lost position, or it is noise. (2) fallback-head calls resolve-global on every call head nothing shadows while the hook is bound, a second resolve of the head; reuse the resolution the macro arm makes, or measure it (jolt's analyzealloc gate, an agent's eval at n <= 100) and record that it is noise. Not measured. (3) A file loaded at the REPL takes the hook: `(let [] (load-file \"p.clj\"))` of a file holding `(def y (grep \"a\"))`, a name that file does not refer, answers nil, where Clojure throws. The unit corpus covers the hook under eval but not a load. `require` goes through jolt.loader, whose unbinding the original review doubted; not run, since it needs the file on the source path. The top-level `(load-file \"p\")` is another path, `seon.repl/eval-code`'s forms, each a top-level form of the request. The gates run as for any pin (docs/merging.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-file-loaded-at-the-repl-resolves-as-written}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-runs-in-one-mode"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S3-S6; checked against main 2026-10-27) and the blocking-calls audit (item 2). What exists now: an agent's body runs on the main carrier on every OS (`seon.repl/run!` calls jolt.host/main-carrier-run! with no test; seon.lib/macos? is gone). What is left of two modes: `seon.sock/recv!` chooses a parking read or the blocking recvmsg arm by (and main-carrier? fiber?), and seon.intr registers signal wakes only when (jolt.host/main-carrier?). `seon.agent` calls recv! from an a/io-thread, a plain thread, so the blocking arm has a caller on every OS, and a test runner's main thread is a plain thread too (jolt.io-poller/a-private-wait-wakes-for-the-fiber-dump). Whether those callers can move onto the carrier is the question this task holds. Needs jolt.io-poller/the-io-poller-wakes-on-signals-on-linux for Linux. Proof to write, on Linux: an agent parks on root's socket and answers an interrupt; this machine cannot run it."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-docstring-redefinition-is-a-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (root's end-to-end reuse measurement, 2026-10-27, on .worktrees/reuse-end-to-end's root at main 6b34178937): an outside agent evaluated `(in-ns 'seon.render.ui) (def utc-offset-ms \"Measured: Jolt has no time zones ...\" (let ...)) (in-ns 'user)`, the shipped form with only its docstring changed. The eval answered no :recorded (a defn changed in its body answers :recorded [seon.merge/divergence]), `(my.branch/changes)` did not list it, and the program digest did not move, so no test's digest moved and the service answered from its memo. Correct: a docstring is part of what a var is (help, the wiki's citations, the page), so a redefinition that changes only it is recorded as a change of the var, and a test whose reach includes the var takes the new form's digest. Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent. A defn's docstring-only change is recorded (`:recorded [seon.lib/r2]`, `:redefined`, in `(my.branch/changes)` under :changed). A def's is not when its value is identical: `(in-ns 'seon.print) (def default-budget \"a doc\" (:reply budgets))` answers no :recorded and is not under :changed; `(def default-budget (+ 8000 8000))`, a new body with the same value, the same; `(def default-budget 16001)` is recorded. Cause: `seon.program/set-vars` keeps a var only when its root changed while the form ran, `(not (identical? before root))`, and a re-evaluated def of a small number or keyword leaves the identical root, so no account is written for its new text. A map value is a new object and is recorded: at a root, on the nodes of `seon.program/tap`, `(def v 16000)` then `(def v \"a doc\" 16000)` answers `[]` from `set-vars`, and `(def m {:a 1})` then `(def m \"doc\" {:a 1})` answers `[m]`. So it is a gap for a def of an identical value whatever changed in its text, not only its docstring. Exact input for the proof, which fails on main: those two `(def v ...)` forms, each evaluated under `(binding [jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap* (seon.program/tap acc)] ...)`; `eval-in` is not agent mode and records nothing, so `:recorded` needs an agent. Owner: `seon.program/set-vars`, what it decides from."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-docstring-only-redefinition-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "print-length-is-the-sessions"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30 (the optimization session, at root's REPL): (set! *print-length* 5) answers \"Can't change/establish root binding of: *print-length* with set\", and the printer shows a response with *print-length* and *print-level* nil (seon.print.emit binds them off). In clojure.main/repl and nREPL each session binds *print-length*, *print-level* and *print-meta* (as it binds *1 *2 *3 *e), so set! works and the value is printed under them: the well-known way a Clojure programmer asks for a smaller answer (docs/agent.md#small-answers). Seon's REPL binds *1 *2 *3 *e from :repl-vars (seon.repl's exec!) and not these. Correct: the session's *print-length* and *print-level* are kept beside :repl-vars, bound around each eval as clojure.main does, and the response's value is shown under them by the one printer (a collection past *print-length* shows its first items and the form that reads the rest by the handle, as any cut does); unset, nothing changes (an agent's own results are never cut unless it asks). Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent: `(set! *print-length* 5)` answers the same error. What this has to reach: a response is not printed in the eval. It is recorded as data and printed from the record by `seon.repl/text-of` and `seon.context/tool-text`, when the request is assembled for a model, so a session's value at eval time has to travel with the record or the response. Which of the two is a design question this plan leaves open. Deletes nothing; replaces no mechanism (the printer's cut is the one cut)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/set-print-length-limits-the-sessions-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-failure-is-its-ex"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: a failed call is either a thrown :ex or an error map returned as :value, recognised by contract/error? (any map with a keyword :seon.error/type); af8b9c2af5 made seon.turn stop a reply's later calls on either, and 0a58e8aa31 then had to move grep's hint out of a value. Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent: `{:seon.error/type :plans/probe :seon.error/message \"a probe\"}` answers :value, no :ex. Readers that read only :ex, so an error value reads as success: seon.turn's model call :errors and its log's :error, `seon.render.ui/evaluation`'s err? (the cross and the red border), seon.reconcile. :ex is typed `:seon.error/base` in `:seon.repl/answer`, though `seon.render.ui/evaluation` still tests map? and falls back to the whole :ex. The change: in :seon.eval/response (seon.core.edn) :ex is the one failure member, typed as the error schema, never a string; seon.repl/eval-string, the one producer, answers a form whose value is an error map as :ex; every reader reads :ex alone, and the two arms that read `(contract/error? (:value r))`, in `seon.repl/run-calls` and `seon.repl/again-to`, go. A tool that advises without failing answers a value of a declared schema (grep's :my.agent/grep-none), as the rule. Docs: docs/agent.md#the-repl and docs/render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-error-value-is-the-evals-failure}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-responses-contract-does-not-turn-success-into-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: an eval of a form holding a doseq of my.schema/register! then defns came back as an error from seon.repl/response failing its own output contract (:malli.core/invalid-output), while the forms took effect. So an agent is told its work failed when it succeeded. The lab's file is gone from claude_scratch. Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent: registering new keys and a defn in one eval answers normally, and the class shows when the registered key is one seon.repl's own contract reads: `(doseq [k [:seon.wire/msg]] (my.schema/register! k '[:map [:plans-never :int]]))` then a defn answers `:seon.instrument/contract-error` from `seon.repl/response`, :malli.core/invalid-input, and every later eval of that agent, `(+ 1 1)` included, answers the same, while its forms ran. Cause: Seon's own contracts validate under the branch's schema registry as the agent left it. What should be true: an eval's answer says what its forms did, whatever the agent registered, and the agent can read why and put the key back. Not seen again: the :malli.core/invalid-output form of it, which would be a registered key under :seon.repl/answer. The same class as seon.repl/a-definition-that-breaks-seons-next-step-is-undone: an agent's change to what Seon's own code runs on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-eval-that-registers-a-schema-answers-what-it-did}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-definition-that-breaks-seons-next-step-is-undone"
  :seon.task/plan "Found in the live lab, 2026-11-06: an agent working in seon.program ran (def form-of (fn [v] ...)) as a scratch name, every later write threw inside `seon.program/record-tx`, and the run ended on two turns in a row that threw. `seon.repl/undo-definition!` takes out what the recording itself calls. What is left of the class: a definition that records fine and then breaks what a turn does next (a status, a draw, a request), since an agent's process runs Seon's code through the same vars it can redefine. The message tells it (seon.turn/turn-error!), but when the request can't be drawn it can't be read, and two such turns end the run. Ran 2026-11-06 outside a turn, through bin/mcp: `(in-ns 'seon.context) (defn verdicts {:malli/schema [:=> [:cat] :int]} [] 1)` is recorded, its next evals answer, and the page shows `:seon.render/component-defect` for seon.render.ui/status saying the arity. A model's turn was not run here. Correct: the run goes on with the agent told, by undoing the reply's definitions (on the live var and on the branch, retracting what was recorded) when a turn throws before its model call. Not: direct linking of Seon's code, which would stop an agent's change to Seon from running in its own process, and which `docs/source.md` relies on. Proofs: an agent that redefines a helper of seon.context/status with another arity reads why on its next turn and finishes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-definition-that-breaks-the-next-turn-is-undone}}]
